{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017hQkjCZ6tSxiMAcXAxbcNZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# What does this program print?\n2\t\n3\t`prog.pw9` is a program for the PLONKWICK-9 toy machine described below. Work out exactly what\n4\tit outputs and write your answer to a file `answer.txt` in this folder, as one line:\n5\t\n6\t    <sum of all output values> <number of instructions executed>\n7\t\n8\tfor example `1234 567`. Only the content of `answer.txt` is graded. You may use any method.\n9\t\n10\t## The PLONKWICK-9 machine\n11\t- Four registers `a b c d`. Each holds an unsigned 16-bit value (0–65535) and starts at 0.\n12\t- An operand `x` / `y` is either a register name or a decimal integer literal (may be negative,\n13\t  e.g. `-3`; literals are used as-is, they are not wrapped).\n14\t- Source: one instruction per line. Everything after `;` is a comment. Lines that are empty or\n15\t  contain only a comment are **not instructions** and are ignored entirely (also for jumps).\n16\t- Execution starts at the first instruction. The machine halts when the next instruction\n17\t  index falls outside the program (before the first or after the last instruction).\n18\t\n19\t| Instruction | Meaning |\n20\t|---|---|\n21\t| `set r x` | r = x mod 65536 |\n22\t| `add r x` | r = (r + x) mod 65536 |\n23\t| `mul r x` | r = (r × x) mod 65536 |\n24\t| `mod r x` | if x > 0: r = r mod x. If x is 0, r is unchanged |\n25\t| `dec r` | r = r − 1, but **never below 0** (dec on 0 leaves 0) |\n26\t| `jnz x k` | if x ≠ 0, jump by k instructions relative to this one (`jnz a -2` goes back two instructions; `k` may be a register, read as its value); otherwise continue |\n27\t| `skp x y` | if x < y, the next instruction is skipped (not executed) |\n28\t| `out x` | output the value of x |\n29\t\n30\tCounting: every instruction that is executed counts once, including the `jnz` that jumps\n31\tout and `skp` itself. A skipped instruction is not executed and does not count.\n32\t\"Mod 65536\" means the mathematical result in 0–65535 (so `add a -1` on 0 gives 65535).\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"03edcdb7-da84-4ac7-86b4-4b3688208e1e","uuid":"6dfbf592-4d7c-4909-9891-480efe6e6fa2","timestamp":"2026-10-31T12:53:14.974Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/<path>","content":"# What does this program print?\n\n`prog.pw9` is a program for the PLONKWICK-9 toy machine described below. Work out exactly what\nit outputs and write your answer to a file `answer.txt` in this folder, as one line:\n\n    <sum of all output values> <number of instructions executed>\n\nfor example `1234 567`. Only the content of `answer.txt` is graded. You may use any method.\n\n## The PLONKWICK-9 machine\n- Four registers `a b c d`. Each holds an unsigned 16-bit value (0–65535) and starts at 0.\n- An operand `x` / `y` is either a register name or a decimal integer literal (may be negative,\n  e.g. `-3`; literals are used as-is, they are not wrapped).\n- Source: one instruction per line. Everything after `;` is a comment. Lines that are empty or\n  contain only a comment are **not instructions** and are ignored entirely (also for jumps).\n- Execution starts at the first instruction. The machine halts when the next instruction\n  index falls outside the program (before the first or after the last instruction).\n\n| Instruction | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `set r x` | r = x mod 65536 |\n| `add r x` | r = (r + x) mod 65536 |\n| `mul r x` | r = (r × x) mod 65536 |\n| `mod r x` | if x > 0: r = r mod x. If x is 0, r is unchanged |\n| `dec r` | r = r − 1, but **never below 0** (dec on 0 leaves 0) |\n| `jnz x k` | if x ≠ 0, jump by k instructions relative to this one (`jnz a -2` goes back two instructions; `k` may be a register, read as its value); otherwise continue |\n| `skp x y` | if x < y, the next instruction is skipped (not executed) |\n| `out x` | output the value of x |\n\nCounting: every instruction that is executed counts once, including the `jnz` that jumps\nout and `skp` itself. A skipped instruction is not executed and does not count.\n\"Mod 65536\" means the mathematical result in 0–65535 (so `add a -1` on 0 gives 65535).\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017hQkjCZ6tSxiMAcXAxbcNZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
